q('Under NICE NG217, which pair contains first-line monotherapy options for focal seizures?', 'Lamotrigine or levetiracetam',[
 ('Ethosuximide or acetazolamide as the standard first pair','Ethosuximide is primarily an absence drug and this is not the stated first-line pair.'),('Only chronic IV lorazepam','A rescue benzodiazepine is not routine long-term first-line focal monotherapy.'),('No treatment can be used unless the EEG is continuously abnormal','Treatment does not require continuous epileptiform EEG.')],
 'Choose a focal regimen after confirming the syndrome', '**Lamotrigine and levetiracetam are common first-line choices in the named guideline.**', ['Comorbidities, pregnancy potential, interactions and titration needs affect selection.','A drug can be unsuitable despite being on a first-line list.'], '**Explain tradeoffs and measure seizure control.**', ['Lamotrigine needs cautious titration.','Levetiracetam can have behavioral adverse effects and needs renal consideration.'], [['Focal seizures','LAM/LEV options'],['Selection','Patient-specific risks'],['Follow-up','Efficacy and tolerability']], [TX])
q('A child has confirmed absence seizures and no other seizure type. Which drug is a standard first-line option?', 'Ethosuximide',[
 ('Carbamazepine','It can aggravate absence seizures.'),('Phenytoin','It is not the appropriate targeted absence treatment.'),('Gabapentin as the usual preferred absence monotherapy','It is not the guideline first-line choice and can be unsuitable.')],
 'Treat the seizure repertoire rather than a generic epilepsy label', '**Ethosuximide is effective for isolated typical absence.**', ['It does not adequately cover every generalized tonic–clonic or myoclonic phenotype.','The presence of other types can favor a broad-spectrum drug.'], '**Reassess if convulsions or morning jerks are reported.**', ['A broader syndrome changes the choice.','Monitor adverse effects and electroclinical response.'], [['Isolated absence','Ethosuximide'],['Absence plus other types','Broader coverage needed'],['Narrow focal drugs','Can worsen absence']], [TX])
q('A person with epilepsy who may become pregnant needs a suitable new ASM. Which safety principle is supported by the AAN–AES–SMFM 2024 guideline?', 'Consider lamotrigine, levetiracetam or oxcarbazepine when appropriate for the syndrome to minimize major-malformation risk',[
 ('Valproate is universally preferred regardless of alternatives or reproductive plans','Its fetal/neurodevelopmental risks require particular avoidance when feasible.'),('Any low-malformation-risk drug is suitable even if it worsens the seizure syndrome','Seizure control and syndrome fit remain essential.'),('No ASM can be used safely enough to treat any pregnancy-capable patient','Untreated seizures also cause harm and several options have a favorable relative profile.')],
 'Optimize both seizure control and reproductive safety before conception', '**The recommendation is conditional on the drug being appropriate for the individual epilepsy.**', ['Avoid valproate when clinically feasible and follow jurisdictional safeguards.','A difficult generalized epilepsy can require specialist discussion rather than abrupt substitution.'], '**Use shared planning instead of an unqualified safest-drug ranking.**', ['Dose, polytherapy and comorbidity matter.','Counsel about folate and pregnancy monitoring.'], [['Lower-risk options','LAM/LEV/OXC when appropriate'],['Valproate','Important fetal risks'],['Primary objective','Safe seizure control']], [PG])
q('A patient with controlled epilepsy discovers an unplanned pregnancy. What is the appropriate medication action?', 'Seek prompt specialist review and continue the current regimen until an individualized safe plan is made, rather than abruptly stopping it',[
 ('Stop every ASM immediately without advice','Withdrawal can provoke dangerous seizures.'),('Double all doses because pregnancy always halves every drug concentration','Pharmacokinetic changes are drug-specific and variable.'),('Ignore pregnancy until delivery','Early counseling and monitoring are important.')],
 'An unsafe abrupt change can harm both patient and fetus', '**The balance of ongoing drug risk and seizure recurrence must be assessed urgently.**', ['Do not switch an effective regimen casually once pregnant.','Valproate and other high-risk exposures require specific counseling.'], '**Plan monitoring through pregnancy and postpartum.**', ['Seizure history and relevant serum levels can guide adjustment.','Review folate, adherence, sleep and rescue plans.'], [['Immediate step','Specialist contact'],['Avoid','Abrupt unsupervised cessation'],['Ongoing care','Individualized monitoring']], [PG], 'clinical_scenario')
q('What minimum daily folic acid supplementation does the AAN–AES–SMFM 2024 guideline recommend for ASM-treated people who may become pregnant?', 'At least 0.4 mg daily before conception and during pregnancy',[
 ('No folate unless anemia develops','The preventive recommendation is not dependent on anemia.'),('One 0.4-mg dose after delivery only','Timing is important before and during pregnancy.'),('Folate guarantees elimination of all ASM fetal risks','It cannot provide that guarantee.')],
 'Use the named guideline’s minimum rather than inventing one universal optimal dose', '**Folate is recommended to reduce neural-tube-defect risk and possibly support neurodevelopment.**', ['Other national protocols may prescribe higher doses in selected settings.','The optimal dose for every epilepsy patient remains uncertain.'], '**Supplementation complements medication optimization.**', ['It does not make a high-risk drug risk-free.','Continue preconception and pregnancy specialist care.'], [['AAN/AES/SMFM minimum','0.4 mg/day'],['Timing','Preconception and pregnancy'],['Limit','Not complete risk elimination']], [PG])
q('Lamotrigine levels fall during pregnancy and seizures recur despite adherence. What mechanism is plausible?', 'Increased lamotrigine clearance during pregnancy',[
 ('Pregnancy always blocks lamotrigine elimination completely','Clearance often increases instead.'),('A lower total level proves intentional nonadherence in every patient','Physiology can explain it.'),('The only explanation is a new structural brain tumor','A medication-exposure change is a relevant alternative.')],
 'A stable dose does not guarantee stable exposure', '**Pregnancy-related clearance can substantially lower lamotrigine concentrations.**', ['Use an individual preconception baseline when available.','Dose adjustments depend on levels, symptoms and the specialist plan.'], '**Plan the postpartum reversal.**', ['Clearance can fall after delivery, making a pregnancy-increased dose toxic if not reviewed.','Monitor rather than applying a fixed multiplier to everyone.'], [['Pregnancy','Often increased clearance'],['Postpartum','Exposure can rise'],['Management','Individual level/clinical review']], [PG,LA], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient on lamotrigine begins an estrogen-containing oral contraceptive. What interaction is important?', 'Estrogen can increase lamotrigine clearance and reduce its concentration',[
 ('Estrogen uniformly doubles lamotrigine levels','The typical direction is the opposite.'),('The interaction affects only the contraceptive and never the ASM','Lamotrigine exposure can change clinically.'),('Stopping estrogen later has no effect on lamotrigine','Concentration can rise when the inducing effect is removed.')],
 'Hormonal therapy can alter seizure-drug exposure', '**The lamotrigine label reports a substantial concentration reduction with estrogen-containing contraceptives.**', ['Pill-free intervals can create fluctuations.','Starting or stopping estrogen may require a planned dose adjustment.'], '**Coordinate contraception and seizure care.**', ['Do not make unsupervised rapid changes.','Assess both seizure control and adverse effects.'], [['Estrogen starts','LAM can fall'],['Estrogen stops','LAM can rise'],['Plan','Clinical/level-guided adjustment']], [LA], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Why do enzyme-inducing ASMs such as carbamazepine matter for hormonal contraception?', 'They can reduce exposure to some contraceptive hormones and compromise effectiveness',[
 ('They guarantee enhanced contraceptive efficacy','Induction can reduce hormone levels.'),('Every contraceptive method is affected identically','Method-specific advice is needed.'),('The interaction means contraception is impossible','Suitable alternatives are available.')],
 'An ASM interaction can change reproductive risk', '**Discuss an effective method that fits the specific drug and local contraceptive guidance.**', ['The interaction is not identical to estrogen’s effect on lamotrigine.','Long-acting and nonhormonal options have different profiles.'], '**Plan proactively.**', ['Review contraceptive changes whenever an inducing drug is started or stopped.','Include emergency-contraception advice when relevant.'], [['Enzyme induction','Can lower hormone exposure'],['Method choice','Drug-specific counseling'],['Medication change','Reassess protection']], [CB,NI])
q('What prescribing step reduces serious lamotrigine rash risk?', 'Use the recommended low initial dose and slow titration, adjusted for interacting drugs such as valproate',[
 ('Start at the full maintenance dose in every patient','High starting doses increase risk.'),('Double the escalation speed when valproate is present','Valproate raises exposure and requires more cautious dosing.'),('Ignore a new rash if seizure control is good','Potential serious reactions require prompt assessment.')],
 'Titration is a safety intervention', '**Stevens–Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis are important rare risks.**', ['Excessive initial dose, rapid escalation and valproate coadministration increase concern.','A restart after a substantial interruption may require retitration.'], '**Give a clear adverse-event plan.**', ['New rash or systemic symptoms need urgent evaluation.','Do not assume every rash is benign or every rash proves the same mechanism.'], [['Initial dose','Low and interaction adjusted'],['Escalation','Slow'],['New rash','Prompt safety assessment']], [LA])
q('What is the principal interaction when valproate is added to lamotrigine?', 'Inhibition of lamotrigine metabolism with increased lamotrigine exposure',[
 ('Rapid induction causing lamotrigine levels to fall','The usual direction is increased exposure.'),('No interaction because both are ASMs','Drug class does not imply pharmacokinetic independence.'),('Complete prevention of every lamotrigine rash','The combination can increase rash risk if dosing is not adjusted.')],
 'A combination may be effective but needs a different dose plan', '**Valproate can more than double lamotrigine concentrations.**', ['Use the product-specific titration/maintenance framework.','Changes in either drug require review of the other.'], '**Monitor clinical toxicity and skin/systemic symptoms.**', ['Dizziness, diplopia and ataxia can signal excessive exposure.','Do not preserve an old dose automatically after an interacting drug change.'], [['Valproate','Metabolic inhibition'],['Lamotrigine','Exposure rises'],['Response','Adjust titration/dose']], [LA])
q('Why can a small phenytoin dose increase cause a disproportionate concentration rise?', 'Saturable metabolism produces nonlinear dose–concentration behavior near the therapeutic range',[
 ('Phenytoin has perfectly linear clearance at every dose','This is not its characteristic kinetics.'),('The serum assay always reads twice the true level after any dose change','That is not the mechanism.'),('Renal filtration is the only elimination pathway','Hepatic metabolism is central.')],
 'A fixed dose increment does not imply a fixed exposure increment', '**As metabolic capacity is approached, clearance behavior becomes nonlinear.**', ['Use cautious adjustments and appropriate timing of level checks.','Drug interactions and liver function can add variability.'], '**Treat the clinical patient as well as the concentration.**', ['Nystagmus, ataxia and dysarthria can indicate toxicity.','A single total level can mislead when binding is altered.'], [['Low-capacity margin','Nonlinear rise'],['Dose change','Cautious increments'],['Toxicity','Clinical plus level assessment']], [PH])
q('A hypoalbuminemic patient has ataxia on phenytoin despite a “normal” total concentration. Which measurement is most helpful?', 'Unbound phenytoin concentration',[
 ('Total concentration alone as definitive proof of safety','A higher free fraction can cause toxicity despite that total.'),('Serum sodium as a direct phenytoin-binding assay','It does not measure active free drug.'),('A normal ALT as proof the dose is safe','It does not establish phenytoin exposure.')],
 'Protein binding separates total from pharmacologically active drug', '**Hypoalbuminemia and renal/hepatic disease can increase the unbound fraction.**', ['Correction equations are imperfect in complex illness.','A direct free level is useful when clinical and total-level findings disagree.'], '**Review other causes of ataxia at the same time.**', ['Assess interactions, dose changes and neurological disease.','Adjust through a monitored plan rather than ignoring symptoms.'], [['Total level','Includes bound drug'],['Free level','Active exposure'],['Low albumin','Greater free fraction']], [PH], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A tube-fed patient has unexpectedly low phenytoin levels after enteral feeding starts. What interaction should be considered?', 'Reduced phenytoin absorption with enteral nutrition',[
 ('Enteral feeds always increase every ASM level','The effect is drug specific.'),('Low levels prove deliberate refusal despite supervised administration','An absorption problem can explain them.'),('The solution is an unmonitored very large dose increase only','This can cause toxicity if feeding conditions later change.')],
 'Drug delivery and feeding schedule can alter effective exposure', '**Phenytoin can have lower concentrations with enteral preparations.**', ['Review formulation, tube technique and timing under the local protocol.','Consider an alternative route or ASM when appropriate.'], '**Recheck after any change.**', ['A previously escalated dose can become excessive when feeds stop.','Clinical seizure control and free-level interpretation remain relevant.'], [['Feeds start','Absorption may fall'],['Feeds stop','Exposure may rise'],['Management','Coordinated administration/monitoring']], [PH], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient develops marked irritability and aggression after levetiracetam initiation. What is the appropriate interpretation?', 'A recognized behavioral adverse effect is possible and warrants medication/psychiatric review',[
 ('Behavioral symptoms can never be drug related','Levetiracetam has recognized behavioral risks.'),('All such symptoms prove ongoing nonconvulsive status','That diagnosis needs appropriate clinical/EEG evidence.'),('Immediately stop all ASMs without a seizure-safety plan','Abrupt withdrawal can provoke recurrence.')],
 'Tolerability includes mood and behavior', '**Review the temporal relation, dose and renal function as well as other causes.**', ['Discuss suicidal or dangerous symptoms urgently.','A different ASM or dose plan may be appropriate.'], '**Preserve seizure protection during changes.**', ['Coordinate a transition rather than unsupervised cessation.','Ask the patient and caregivers about behavioral changes during follow-up.'], [['LEV','Behavioral effects possible'],['Safety','Assess severity promptly'],['Change','Supervised seizure plan']], [NI,ref('US Keppra Prescribing Information','https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=3ca9df05-a506-4ec8-a4fe-320f1219ab21')], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A topiramate-treated patient develops renal stones and hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. What mechanism is relevant?', 'Carbonic anhydrase inhibition with renal bicarbonate loss',[
 ('Pure hepatic copper chelation','This is not topiramate’s mechanism.'),('Obligate severe lactic acidosis from tissue hypoxia','The stated normal-gap pattern points to bicarbonate loss.'),('A pharmacological guarantee that stones cannot occur','Topiramate increases stone risk.')],
 'One ancillary drug action can create a predictable adverse-effect pattern', '**Bicarbonate loss can cause a non-anion-gap acidosis.**', ['Other carbonic-anhydrase inhibitors or a ketogenic diet can increase concern.','Renal function affects dosing.'], '**Review bicarbonate, symptoms and ongoing need.**', ['Hydration and a specialist dose/alternative plan may be appropriate.','Cognitive slowing and reproductive risks are separate important counseling points.'], [['Mechanism','Carbonic anhydrase inhibition'],['Biochemistry','Bicarbonate loss'],['Complication','Stones/acidosis']], [TP], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Before carbamazepine in a patient with ancestry where HLA-B*15:02 is prevalent, what test is relevant?', 'HLA-B*15:02 testing to assess serious cutaneous-reaction risk',[
 ('HFE C282Y testing as the specific rash predictor','This assesses a different inherited disorder.'),('A normal sodium as definitive exclusion of SJS/TEN risk','Sodium does not determine HLA-associated risk.'),('No testing because ancestry and genotype can never affect drug reactions','They can materially affect risk.')],
 'Use pharmacogenetics for a defined drug-risk relationship', '**HLA-B*15:02 is associated with carbamazepine-related SJS/TEN in relevant populations.**', ['A positive result generally favors avoiding the drug unless exceptional benefit outweighs risk.','A negative test does not remove every hypersensitivity possibility.'], '**Continue ordinary clinical vigilance.**', ['Counsel about rash and systemic symptoms.','Other HLA variants and adverse effects have different implications.'], [['Risk allele','HLA-B*15:02'],['Outcome concern','SJS/TEN'],['Negative result','Not zero overall risk']], [CB])
q('An older patient on carbamazepine develops confusion with sodium 122 mmol/L. What drug-related mechanism is plausible?', 'Carbamazepine-associated hyponatremia through an antidiuretic/SIADH-like effect',[
 ('A guaranteed sodium rise from every ASM','The actual direction is low sodium.'),('Normal neurological aging without a reversible cause','Severe hyponatremia is clinically important.'),('Only a phenytoin protein-binding effect','That does not explain this electrolyte abnormality.')],
 'A seizure medicine can create a metabolic cause of seizures or confusion', '**Older age and concomitant diuretics can increase hyponatremia risk.**', ['Assess volume status and other causes.','Oxcarbazepine can also be associated with significant hyponatremia.'], '**Treat symptomatic sodium disturbance carefully.**', ['Correction rate and seizure severity require monitored management.','Review the ASM without abrupt unplanned withdrawal.'], [['Drug effect','Antidiuretic water retention'],['Symptoms','Confusion/seizures possible'],['Management','Controlled correction and medication review']], [CB], 'clinical_scenario')
